-- 1. [Balancing Equations (incl. redox) · Skill 2 · ans A] id 94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Glycerol, \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3\) (molar mass \(92.0\,\text{g/mol}\)), undergoes complete combustion to \(\text{CO}_2\) and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) according to the unbalanced skeleton \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). After the equation is balanced with the smallest whole-number coefficients, a burner combusts \(9.2\,\text{g}\) of glycerol completely. The mass of \(\text{O}_2\) consumed is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(11.2\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(16.0\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(9.6\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(12.8\\,\\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the equation must first be balanced and then applied through a mass-to-mass chain. Balance carbon first: the \(3\) carbon atoms in one \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3\) require \(3\,\text{CO}_2\). Balance hydrogen next: the \(8\) hydrogen atoms require \(4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (since \(8 \div 2 = 4\)). Now balance oxygen by counting both product oxygens against the oxygen already inside glycerol: the product side holds \((3 \times 2) + (4 \times 1) = 6 + 4 = 10\) oxygen atoms, but \(3\) of these are already supplied by the glycerol itself, leaving \(10 - 3 = 7\) oxygen atoms that must come from \(\text{O}_2\); \(7 \div 2 = 3.5\), a fractional coefficient. Multiplying the entire equation by \(2\) to reach the smallest whole numbers gives \(2\,\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3 + 7\,\text{O}_2 \rightarrow 6\,\text{CO}_2 + 8\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), so the controlling \(\text{O}_2\):glycerol ratio is \(7:2 = 3.5\). The \(9.2\,\text{g}\) of glycerol is \(9.2 \div 92.0 = 0.10\,\text{mol}\); this consumes \(0.10 \times 3.5 = 0.35\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{O}_2\), which is \(0.35 \times 32.0 = 11.2\,\text{g}\) \(\text{O}_2\). (Choice B) A value of \(16.0\,\text{g}\) arises from ignoring the \(3\) oxygen atoms already contained in glycerol and balancing oxygen as if all \(10\) product oxygens must be supplied by \(\text{O}_2\), giving \(2\,\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3 + 10\,\text{O}_2\) and an inflated ratio of \(10:2 = 5\); then \(0.10 \times 5 \times 32.0 = 16.0\,\text{g}\). (Choice C) A value of \(9.6\,\text{g}\) results from substituting the \(\text{CO}_2\) coefficient for the \(\text{O}_2\) coefficient, using a ratio of \(6:2 = 3\) instead of \(7:2\); then \(0.10 \times 3 \times 32.0 = 9.6\,\text{g}\). (Choice D) A value of \(12.8\,\text{g}\) results from substituting the \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) coefficient for the \(\text{O}_2\) coefficient, using a ratio of \(8:2 = 4\); then \(0.10 \times 4 \times 32.0 = 12.8\,\text{g}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a correctly balanced redox/combustion equation, account for the oxygen carried within the fuel as the controlling constraint, and propagate an intermediate mole quantity through a molar-mass conversion to reach the final mass.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Drops the controlling constraint by ignoring the 3 oxygen atoms inside the glycerol fuel, balancing O2 as if it must supply all product oxygen (ratio 5 instead of 3.5)$a$),
  ('94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Uses the CO2 coefficient (6 per 2 fuel = ratio 3) in place of the O2 coefficient in the mole-ratio step$a$),
  ('94a0ba15-f82b-4fab-b4a2-edd8a75fc1c5', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Uses the H2O coefficient (8 per 2 fuel = ratio 4) in place of the O2 coefficient in the mole-ratio step$a$);

-- 2. [Balancing Equations (incl. redox) · Skill 2 · ans B] id 9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In acidic solution, dichromate oxidizes ethanol to acetic acid by the unbalanced skeleton \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + \text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Cr}^{3+} + \text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + \text{H}_2\text{O}\), with the chromium reduced to \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\). When this equation is balanced to the smallest whole-number coefficients, the coefficient on \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(7\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(11\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(14\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(22\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the half-reaction method, applied with the correct electron count for the organic oxidation, yields \(\text{H}_2\text{O} = 11\). First build the reduction half: \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 14\,\text{H}^+ + 6\,e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 7\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (a \(6\)-electron half because the two \(\text{Cr}\) drop from \(+6\) to \(+3\)). Next build the oxidation half by assigning carbon oxidation states: in \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH}\) the \(\text{CH}_3\) carbon is \(-3\) and the \(\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) carbon is \(-1\); in \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) the \(\text{CH}_3\) carbon stays \(-3\) while the carboxyl carbon rises to \(+3\). Only the \(\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) carbon changes, from \(-1\) to \(+3\), so each ethanol loses \(4\) electrons: \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + 4\,\text{H}^+ + 4\,e^-\). To cancel electrons, take the least common multiple of \(6\) and \(4\), which is \(12\): multiply the reduction half by \(2\) and the oxidation half by \(3\). Adding gives \(2\,\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 28\,\text{H}^+ + 3\,\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 4\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 14\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + 12\,\text{H}^+\). Cancelling the \(12\,\text{H}^+\) common to both sides (\(28-12=16\)) and the \(3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) common to both sides (\(14-3=11\)) gives the reduced equation \(2\,\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 16\,\text{H}^+ + 3\,\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} \rightarrow 4\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 11\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\), which conserves every atom (\(\text{Cr}\) \(4=4\), \(\text{O}\) \(17=17\), \(\text{H}\) \(34=34\), \(\text{C}\) \(6=6\)) and charge (\(+12=+12\)), so \(\text{H}_2\text{O} = 11\). (Choice A) A coefficient of \(7\) results from stopping the oxidation at acetaldehyde, \(\text{CH}_3\text{CHO}\), a \(2\)-electron oxidation (\(\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{CHO} + 2\,\text{H}^+ + 2\,e^-\)); the least common multiple of \(2\) and \(6\) is then \(6\), so only one dichromate half is needed and the balanced equation \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 8\,\text{H}^+ + 3\,\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 7\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3\,\text{CH}_3\text{CHO}\) carries seven waters, but the stated product is the carboxylic acid, not the aldehyde. (Choice C) A coefficient of \(14\) is the water count on the product side before the spectator water is cancelled; the summed equation \(2\,\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 28\,\text{H}^+ + 3\,\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 4\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 14\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + 12\,\text{H}^+\) shows \(14\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) only because the \(3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) appearing on the left has not yet been subtracted from both sides. (Choice D) A coefficient of \(22\) arises from cross-multiplying the two halves by each other's electron count (reduction by \(4\), oxidation by \(6\)) instead of by the least common multiple, producing \(4\,\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 32\,\text{H}^+ + 6\,\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH} \rightarrow 8\,\text{Cr}^{3+} + 22\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 6\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\); this is balanced but every coefficient is exactly twice the smallest whole-number set, so it has not been reduced. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must assign carbon oxidation states to fix the electron count of the organic half-reaction, combine it with the dichromate half through their least common multiple, and cancel spectator species before reading off the final coefficient.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Oxidation stopped one step early at the aldehyde (CH3CHO), treating ethanol as a 2-electron oxidation instead of the 4-electron oxidation to the carboxylic acid, which changes the LCM from 12 to 6.$a$),
  ('9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Correct 4-electron path but failed to cancel the spectator water that appears on both sides, reporting the uncancelled product-side count (14) instead of the net coefficient (11).$a$),
  ('9b9ade90-95ef-44ee-8383-6e9303b588f5', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Combined the half-reactions by cross-multiplying each by the other's electron count (6 and 4) instead of by their least common multiple (12), giving an equation that balances but is exactly 2x the smallest whole-number set.$a$);

-- 3. [Balancing Equations (incl. redox) · Skill 2 · ans D] id b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(1.00\,\text{g}\) iron ore sample is dissolved and all of its iron is converted to \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\), which is then titrated in acidic solution with \(\text{KMnO}_4\) by the unbalanced skeleton \(\text{MnO}_4^- + \text{Fe}^{2+} + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + \text{Fe}^{3+} + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). The faint purple endpoint appears after \(20.0\,\text{mL}\) of \(0.0500\,\text{M}\) \(\text{KMnO}_4\) has been added. Taking the molar mass of iron as \(56.0\,\text{g/mol}\), the mass percent of iron in the ore is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(5.6\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(33.6\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(14.0\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(28.0\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the electron balance fixes the reacting ratio, and that ratio then drives the entire mass calculation. Manganese is reduced from \(+7\) in \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\), gaining 5 electrons, while each iron is oxidized from \(+2\) to \(+3\), losing 1 electron; to equalize electrons transferred, the balanced equation is \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 5\,\text{Fe}^{2+} + 8\,\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 5\,\text{Fe}^{3+} + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), giving a \(1{:}5\) ratio of permanganate to iron(II). The moles of permanganate delivered are \(0.0200\,\text{L} \times 0.0500\,\text{M} = 1.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). Applying the \(1{:}5\) ratio, the moles of \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) titrated are \(5 \times 1.00\times10^{-3} = 5.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). The mass of iron is \(5.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol} \times 56.0\,\text{g/mol} = 0.280\,\text{g}\), so the mass percent is \(\frac{0.280\,\text{g}}{1.00\,\text{g}} \times 100\% = 28.0\%\). (Choice A) \(5.6\%\) comes from skipping the electron balance entirely and assuming a \(1{:}1\) mole ratio, giving \(1.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) Fe; this ignores that each permanganate accepts 5 electrons while each iron(II) supplies only 1. (Choice B) \(33.6\%\) results from treating the permanganate as if it gained 6 electrons—the value that applies to dichromate, \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\), not to \(\text{MnO}_4^-\)—which yields an incorrect \(1{:}6\) ratio and \(6.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) Fe; permanganate manganese goes \(+7 \rightarrow +2\), a 5-electron change, so the correct ratio is \(1{:}5\). (Choice C) \(14.0\%\) arises from counting the iron oxidation as a 2-electron loss (as if \(\text{Fe}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{3+}\) released two electrons), producing a \(1{:}2.5\) ratio and \(2.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) Fe; the change from \(+2\) to \(+3\) is only a 1-electron loss. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must balance the half-reaction electron transfer to establish the reacting ratio, then chain that ratio through titration moles and molar mass to reach a mass percent.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Omits electron balancing and uses a default 1:1 mole ratio$a$),
  ('b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Assigns permanganate the 6-electron change that belongs to dichromate, giving a 1:6 ratio$a$),
  ('b05423f5-dc13-4d29-aab9-332b538d2719', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Counts Fe2+ to Fe3+ as a 2-electron loss, producing a 1:2.5 ratio$a$);

-- 4. [Empirical & Molecular Formula + Percent Composition · Skill 2 · ans D] id 51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A volatile liquid containing only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen is analyzed by combustion. A \(4.30\ \text{g}\) sample burns completely to give \(8.80\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{CO}_2\) and \(2.70\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), and a separate vapor-density measurement fixes its molar mass at \(86\ \text{g/mol}\). Using \(\text{C}=12.0\), \(\text{H}=1.0\), \(\text{O}=16.0\), \(\text{CO}_2=44.0\), and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}=18.0\ \text{g/mol}\), which molecular formula is most consistent with these data?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}_8\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_4\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_3\\text{O}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_9\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_6\\text{O}_2\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the combustion data fix the moles of carbon and hydrogen, oxygen must be recovered by mass difference, and only then can the empirical formula be scaled by the molar mass to the molecular formula. All of the carbon ends up in \(\text{CO}_2\): \(\frac{8.80}{44.0} = 0.200\ \text{mol CO}_2\) carries \(0.200\ \text{mol C}\), a mass of \(0.200 \times 12.0 = 2.40\ \text{g C}\). All of the hydrogen ends up in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), but each water molecule holds two hydrogen atoms: \(\frac{2.70}{18.0} = 0.150\ \text{mol H}_2\text{O}\) carries \(2 \times 0.150 = 0.300\ \text{mol H}\), a mass of \(0.300 \times 1.0 = 0.30\ \text{g H}\). Because the compound contains oxygen, the oxygen mass cannot be measured from a combustion product (the \(\text{O}_2\) supplied confounds it) and must be found by difference: \(4.30 - 2.40 - 0.30 = 1.60\ \text{g O}\), which is \(\frac{1.60}{16.0} = 0.100\ \text{mol O}\). The mole ratio \(0.200 : 0.300 : 0.100\) divides by \(0.100\) to give \(2 : 3 : 1\), so the empirical formula is \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}\) with empirical-formula mass \(2(12.0)+3(1.0)+16.0 = 43.0\ \text{g/mol}\). The scaling multiplier is \(n = \frac{86}{43.0} = 2\), so the molecular formula is \((\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\text{O})_2 = \text{C}_4\text{H}_6\text{O}_2\) (formula mass \(86\ \text{g/mol}\)), consistent with the measured molar mass. (Choice A) \(\text{C}_8\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_4\) preserves the correct \(2:3:1\) ratio but applies a multiplier of \(n = 4\) instead of \(n = 2\), as would arise from dividing the molar mass by half the empirical-formula mass (\(\frac{86}{21.5} = 4\)); its formula mass is \(172\ \text{g/mol}\), twice the measured value. (Choice B) \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\text{O}\) is the correct empirical formula, but its formula mass is only \(43\ \text{g/mol}\), half the measured \(86\ \text{g/mol}\); this choice omits the final molar-mass scaling step, reporting the simplest ratio rather than the actual molecule. (Choice C) \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_9\) results from treating the sample as a pure hydrocarbon and ignoring the oxygen-by-difference step; using only the \(0.200\ \text{mol C}\) and \(0.300\ \text{mol H}\) gives a \(2:3\) ratio and an empirical unit \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_3\) (mass \(27\ \text{g/mol}\)), which scaled toward \(86\ \text{g/mol}\) rounds to a tripled \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_9\); the \(1.60\ \text{g}\) of oxygen present in the compound has been dropped. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain combustion stoichiometry, an oxygen-by-difference mass balance, empirical-formula reduction, and molar-mass scaling, carrying each intermediate result into the next step.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Doubles the scaling multiplier by dividing the molar mass by half the empirical-formula mass$a$),
  ('51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Reports the correct empirical formula but stops before scaling by the molar mass to reach the molecular formula$a$),
  ('51d3f392-234a-4d72-b0c4-1342c0d8253c', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Drops the oxygen-by-difference constraint, treating the oxygen-containing compound as a pure hydrocarbon$a$);

-- 5. [Empirical & Molecular Formula + Percent Composition · Skill 2 · ans B] id 7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Washing soda is sodium carbonate hydrate, \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\cdot n\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). When a \(28.6\,\text{g}\) sample of the pure hydrate is heated until all the water of hydration is driven off, \(10.6\,\text{g}\) of anhydrous \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\) remains. Using atomic weights \(\text{Na}=22.99\), \(\text{C}=12.01\), \(\text{O}=16.00\), and \(\text{H}=1.008\,\text{g/mol}\), the percent by mass of oxygen in the original hydrate is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(45.3\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(72.7\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(63.0\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(55.9\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the hydration number must first be found from the mass loss, and only then can every oxygen atom in the full hydrate be totaled against the hydrate's molar mass. Step 1: the water driven off is \(28.6 - 10.6 = 18.0\,\text{g}\). The anhydrous \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\) has molar mass \(2(22.99) + 12.01 + 3(16.00) = 105.99\,\text{g/mol}\), so the residue is \(10.6/105.99 = 0.100\,\text{mol}\). One \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) is \(2(1.008) + 16.00 = 18.02\,\text{g/mol}\), so the water lost is \(18.0/18.02 = 1.00\,\text{mol}\). The mole ratio \(1.00/0.100 = 10\) fixes \(n = 10\), i.e. \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\cdot 10\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). Step 2: this formula unit has \(3\) oxygens in the carbonate plus \(10\) oxygens from the ten waters, for \(13\) oxygen atoms total, a mass of \(13 \times 16.00 = 208.0\,\text{g}\). The molar mass of the hydrate is \(105.99 + 10(18.02) = 286.15\,\text{g/mol}\). The mass percent of oxygen is \(\dfrac{208.0}{286.15}\times 100\% = 72.7\%\). (Choice A) \(45.3\%\) is the mass percent of oxygen in the anhydrous \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\) alone, \(\dfrac{3(16.00)}{105.99}\times 100\%\), which discards the water of hydration entirely instead of using the \(n = 10\) obtained from the mass loss. (Choice C) \(63.0\%\) is the mass percent of water in the hydrate, \(\dfrac{10(18.02)}{286.15}\times 100\%\), which halts at the natural intermediate quantity and reports the water fraction rather than carrying through to the oxygen fraction the question requests. (Choice D) \(55.9\%\) counts only the ten oxygens contributed by the water molecules, \(\dfrac{10(16.00)}{286.15}\times 100\%\), dropping the three oxygens carried by the carbonate ion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a gravimetric determination of the hydration number to a percent-composition calculation, combining the carbonate and water oxygens over the full hydrate molar mass.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Computes mass percent of oxygen in only the anhydrous salt, ignoring the water of hydration that the mass-loss data established$a$),
  ('7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops one step early at the percent-water-by-mass intermediate instead of carrying through to percent oxygen$a$),
  ('7980d016-6e22-4b0e-a53d-ad43786f7e33', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Counts only the water-derived oxygens and forgets the three oxygens in the carbonate ion (dropped constituent)$a$);

-- 6. [Empirical & Molecular Formula + Percent Composition · Skill 2 · ans D] id 7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A pure stimulant alkaloid contains only carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen and is found by combustion analysis to be \(49.5\%\) C, \(5.2\%\) H, and \(28.9\%\) N by mass, with the remaining mass being oxygen (atomic masses: \(\text{C} = 12.01\), \(\text{H} = 1.008\), \(\text{N} = 14.01\), \(\text{O} = 16.00\,\text{g/mol}\)). A separate measurement gives its molar mass as approximately \(194\,\text{g/mol}\). The molecular formula most consistent with these data is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_5\\text{N}_2\\text{O}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{C}_8\\text{H}_{10}\\text{N}_4\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{C}_{16}\\text{H}_{20}\\text{N}_8\\text{O}_4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{C}_8\\text{H}_{10}\\text{N}_4\\text{O}_2\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the molecular formula is obtained by first deriving the empirical formula from percent composition and then scaling it with the measured molar mass. The oxygen percentage is not given and must be found by difference: \(100\% - 49.5\% - 5.2\% - 28.9\% = 16.4\%\) O. Taking a \(100\,\text{g}\) sample and dividing each element's mass by its atomic mass gives moles: \(\text{C} = 49.5/12.01 = 4.12\), \(\text{H} = 5.2/1.008 = 5.16\), \(\text{N} = 28.9/14.01 = 2.06\), and \(\text{O} = 16.4/16.00 = 1.03\,\text{mol}\). Dividing every value by the smallest \((1.03)\) yields \(\text{C}:\text{H}:\text{N}:\text{O} = 4.0 : 5.0 : 2.0 : 1.0\), so the empirical formula is \(\text{C}_4\text{H}_5\text{N}_2\text{O}\) with an empirical mass of \(4(12.01) + 5(1.008) + 2(14.01) + 16.00 = 97.1\,\text{g/mol}\). The whole-number multiplier linking the empirical and molecular formulas is \(194/97.1 \approx 2\), so each subscript is multiplied by \(2\) to give \(\text{C}_8\text{H}_{10}\text{N}_4\text{O}_2\), whose formula mass \((194.2\,\text{g/mol})\) matches the measured molar mass. (Choice A) \(\text{C}_4\text{H}_5\text{N}_2\text{O}\) is the correct empirical formula, but it is reported without applying the molar-mass scaling step; its mass of \(97.1\,\text{g/mol}\) is only half the measured \(194\,\text{g/mol}\), so the empirical formula must be doubled to reach the molecular formula. (Choice B) \(\text{C}_8\text{H}_{10}\text{N}_4\) results from omitting oxygen entirely rather than finding it by difference; computing the ratio from only C, H, and N gives empirical \(\text{C}_4\text{H}_5\text{N}_2\) (mass \(81.1\,\text{g/mol}\)) which when doubled gives a formula mass of only \(162\,\text{g/mol}\), inconsistent with the measured \(194\,\text{g/mol}\). (Choice C) \(\text{C}_{16}\text{H}_{20}\text{N}_8\text{O}_4\) results from applying the multiplier of \(2\) a second time, as if a fractional subscript still needed clearing; its formula mass of \(388\,\text{g/mol}\) is twice the measured molar mass, so the doubling was performed once too often. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain percent-composition analysis (with oxygen by difference), empirical-formula determination, empirical-mass calculation, and molar-mass scaling, carrying each intermediate result into the next step.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Derives the correct empirical formula C4H5N2O but stops there, reporting it as the molecular formula without using the measured molar mass to find the scaling multiplier (skips the empirical-to-molecular step).$a$),
  ('7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Drops the controlling constraint by failing to compute oxygen by difference; builds the mole ratio from only C, H, and N (empirical C4H5N2) and scales that oxygen-free formula by two.$a$),
  ('7c2121ec-877b-4260-8cb0-201aa14c3b79', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Applies the empirical-to-molecular multiplier twice (doubling the already-correct C8H10N4O2), as if a fractional subscript still needed to be cleared, producing a formula mass of twice the measured molar mass.$a$);

-- 7. [Empirical & Molecular Formula + Percent Composition · Skill 2 · ans A] id a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Complete combustion of a 1.10 g sample of a compound containing carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen yields 2.20 g of \(\text{CO}_2\) and 0.901 g of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\); a separate measurement gives the compound's molar mass as 88.0 g/mol. Using molar masses \(\text{CO}_2 = 44.01\), \(\text{H}_2\text{O} = 18.02\), \(\text{C} = 12.01\), \(\text{H} = 1.008\), and \(\text{O} = 16.00\ \text{g/mol}\), the molecular formula of the compound is most likely which of the following?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_8\\text{O}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4\\text{O}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_8\\text{O}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the oxygen content must be found by difference and the empirical formula must then be scaled to the measured molar mass. All carbon ends up in \(\text{CO}_2\): \(\text{mol C} = 2.20\ \text{g} \div 44.01\ \text{g/mol} = 0.0500\ \text{mol}\), so \(\text{mass C} = 0.0500 \times 12.01 = 0.600\ \text{g}\). All hydrogen ends up in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), with two H per molecule: \(\text{mol H} = 2 \times (0.901\ \text{g} \div 18.02\ \text{g/mol}) = 2 \times 0.0500 = 0.100\ \text{mol}\), so \(\text{mass H} = 0.100 \times 1.008 = 0.101\ \text{g}\). Because the compound also contains oxygen, the oxygen mass cannot be read from a product and must be obtained by difference: \(\text{mass O} = 1.10 - 0.600 - 0.101 = 0.399\ \text{g}\), giving \(\text{mol O} = 0.399 \div 16.00 = 0.0249\ \text{mol}\). Dividing all three by the smallest value \(0.0249\) gives \(\text{C}:\text{H}:\text{O} = 2.0 : 4.0 : 1.0\), so the empirical formula is \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\text{O}\) with empirical mass \(2(12.01) + 4(1.008) + 16.00 = 44.05\ \text{g/mol}\). The measured molar mass divided by the empirical mass is \(88.0 \div 44.05 = 2.00\), so the molecular formula is twice the empirical unit, \(\text{C}_4\text{H}_8\text{O}_2\). (Choice B) \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\text{O}\) is the correct empirical formula but not the molecular formula; it results from stopping after the element ratio and omitting the final step of dividing the molar mass (88.0) by the empirical mass (44.05) to obtain the scaling factor of 2. (Choice C) \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\) results from ignoring oxygen and treating the sample as a pure hydrocarbon: using only the \(0.0500\ \text{mol C}\) and \(0.100\ \text{mol H}\) gives a \(1:2\) ratio (\(\text{CH}_2\), empirical mass 14.03), and forcing \(88.0 \div 14.03\) to a whole number yields \(6\); the non-integer quotient of 6.27 is itself the signal that the oxygen mass was never accounted for by difference. (Choice D) \(\text{C}_4\text{H}_8\text{O}\) results from a scale error in the oxygen step, dividing the oxygen mass by 32 g/mol (the mass of \(\text{O}_2\)) instead of 16.00 g/mol for atomic O; this halves the oxygen moles to \(0.0125\), shifting the ratio to \(4:8:1\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a combustion-analysis mole calculation with an oxygen-by-difference determination and then convert the empirical formula to a molecular formula using the measured molar mass.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Reports the correctly derived empirical formula as the molecular formula, omitting the molar-mass scaling step (n = MM/empirical mass = 2).$a$),
  ('a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Drops the controlling constraint that the compound contains oxygen; treats it as a pure hydrocarbon, computes CH2, and forces the molar-mass ratio to a whole number.$a$),
  ('a5cdd5c7-5bf6-4ed9-a8a2-0e55622273a6', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Divides the oxygen mass by the molar mass of O2 (32 g/mol) instead of atomic O (16 g/mol), halving the oxygen moles and skewing the element ratio to 4:8:1.$a$);

-- 8. [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield · Skill 4 · ans A] id 6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Magnesium burns in nitrogen by the balanced equation \(3\,\text{Mg} + \text{N}_2 \rightarrow \text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\). A reactor is charged with \(14.58\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{Mg}\) (\(24.3\,\text{g/mol}\)) and \(9.80\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{N}_2\) (\(28.0\,\text{g/mol}\)), and after the reaction goes to completion \(16.14\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) (\(100.9\,\text{g/mol}\)) is isolated. The percent yield of \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(80\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(46\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(27\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(125\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the theoretical yield must be computed from the limiting reactant before the percent yield can be found, and that chain gives \(80\%\). First convert each charge to moles: \(14.58\,\text{g} \div 24.3\,\text{g/mol} = 0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}\) and \(9.80\,\text{g} \div 28.0\,\text{g/mol} = 0.350\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{N}_2\). Dividing each by its coefficient in \(3\,\text{Mg} + \text{N}_2 \rightarrow \text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) gives \(0.600 \div 3 = 0.200\) for \(\text{Mg}\) versus \(0.350 \div 1 = 0.350\) for \(\text{N}_2\); the smaller value identifies \(\text{Mg}\) as the limiting reactant. The \(3:1\) ratio of \(\text{Mg}\) to \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) then sets the theoretical amount at \(0.600 \div 3 = 0.200\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\), or \(0.200\,\text{mol} \times 100.9\,\text{g/mol} = 20.18\,\text{g}\). The percent yield is the actual mass over this theoretical mass: \(16.14\,\text{g} \div 20.18\,\text{g} \times 100\% \approx 80\%\). (Choice B) Mis-assigning \(\text{N}_2\) as limiting (for example by comparing raw moles, \(0.350\) versus the unscaled \(0.600\), or by choosing the larger starting mass) gives \(0.350\,\text{mol} \times 100.9 = 35.3\,\text{g}\) theoretical and \(16.14 \div 35.3 \approx 46\%\); this reverses the limiting-reactant result because it omits the coefficients. (Choice C) Carrying \(\text{Mg}\) forward as the limiting reactant but skipping the \(3:1\) ratio—treating \(0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}\) as \(0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) (\(60.5\,\text{g}\) theoretical)—inflates the denominator and yields \(16.14 \div 60.5 \approx 27\%\). (Choice D) Computing the theoretical and actual masses correctly but inverting the percent-yield ratio (\(20.18 \div 16.14 \times 100\%\)) returns \(125\%\), a value above \(100\%\) that is physically impossible for a yield and signals the ratio was flipped. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must chain the limiting-reactant determination into a theoretical-yield calculation and then into a percent-yield ratio, carrying each intermediate result into the next step.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Selects the wrong limiting reactant by comparing raw moles or starting masses without dividing by coefficients, then propagates 0.350 mol into the yield$a$),
  ('6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Correctly identifies Mg as limiting but drops the 3:1 mole ratio in the theoretical-yield step, equating 0.600 mol Mg with 0.600 mol product$a$),
  ('6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Inverts the percent-yield ratio (theoretical over actual instead of actual over theoretical), producing an impossible value above 100%$a$);

-- 9. [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield · Skill 2 · ans B] id 6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Aluminum reacts with bromine by the balanced equation \(2\,\text{Al} + 3\,\text{Br}_2 \rightarrow 2\,\text{AlBr}_3\). A vessel is charged with \(10.8\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{Al}\) (molar mass \(27\,\text{g/mol}\)) and \(72.0\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{Br}_2\) (molar mass \(160\,\text{g/mol}\)), but the reaction stalls once \(80\%\) of the limiting reactant has been consumed. What mass of the excess reactant remains when the reaction stops?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(2.70\\,\\text{g}\\) of \\(\\text{Al}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(4.32\\,\\text{g}\\) of \\(\\text{Al}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(1.08\\,\\text{g}\\) of \\(\\text{Al}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(14.4\\,\\text{g}\\) of \\(\\text{Br}_2\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the limiting reactant must first be identified by dividing each amount by its coefficient, then the \(80\%\) conversion and the \(2:3\) mole ratio are chained to find how much excess \(\text{Al}\) survives. Convert each mass to moles: \(10.8\,\text{g} \div 27\,\text{g/mol} = 0.40\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Al}\) and \(72.0\,\text{g} \div 160\,\text{g/mol} = 0.45\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Br}_2\). Although \(\text{Br}_2\) is present in the larger molar amount, the limiting reactant is set by moles divided by coefficient: \(0.40 \div 2 = 0.20\) for \(\text{Al}\) versus \(0.45 \div 3 = 0.15\) for \(\text{Br}_2\); the smaller value identifies \(\text{Br}_2\) as limiting and \(\text{Al}\) as the excess reactant. Because the reaction stops at \(80\%\) consumption of the limiting reactant, only \(0.80 \times 0.45\,\text{mol} = 0.36\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Br}_2\) actually reacts. The \(2:3\) ratio of \(\text{Al}\) to \(\text{Br}_2\) means \(\text{Al}\) consumed \(= 0.36\,\text{mol}\,\text{Br}_2 \times \frac{2}{3} = 0.24\,\text{mol}\). The \(\text{Al}\) remaining is \(0.40\,\text{mol} - 0.24\,\text{mol} = 0.16\,\text{mol}\), which equals \(0.16\,\text{mol} \times 27\,\text{g/mol} = 4.32\,\text{g}\). (Choice A) \(2.70\,\text{g}\) results from dropping the \(80\%\) constraint and assuming the limiting reactant is fully consumed: at \(100\%\) conversion all \(0.45\,\text{mol}\,\text{Br}_2\) reacts, consuming \(0.30\,\text{mol}\,\text{Al}\) and leaving \(0.10\,\text{mol} = 2.70\,\text{g}\), which overstates the extent of reaction the stem specifies. (Choice C) \(1.08\,\text{g}\) results from applying a \(1:1\) mole ratio instead of the equation's \(2:3\) ratio: treating \(0.36\,\text{mol}\,\text{Br}_2\) as consuming \(0.36\,\text{mol}\,\text{Al}\) leaves \(0.04\,\text{mol} = 1.08\,\text{g}\), but the balanced coefficients require \(\text{Al}\) and \(\text{Br}_2\) to react in a \(2:3\) ratio, not \(1:1\). (Choice D) \(14.4\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{Br}_2\) reports the unreacted portion of the limiting reactant rather than the excess reactant: \(0.45\,\text{mol} - 0.36\,\text{mol} = 0.09\,\text{mol}\,\text{Br}_2 = 14.4\,\text{g}\), but the question asks for the leftover excess species, which is \(\text{Al}\), not the \(\text{Br}_2\) that was incompletely consumed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the limiting-reactant rule, a percent-conversion constraint, and the balanced mole ratio across several dependent steps to reach the leftover mass of the correct species.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Dropped the 80%-conversion controlling constraint and computed the excess remaining at complete (100%) consumption of the limiting reactant$a$),
  ('6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Used a 1:1 mole ratio in the stoichiometry step instead of the balanced 2:3 ratio when converting reacted Br2 to Al consumed$a$),
  ('6df2eeae-1dc2-42df-adbd-73a55e62e5f2', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reported the unreacted leftover of the limiting reactant (Br2) instead of the excess reactant (Al) that the question requests$a$);

-- 10. [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield · Skill 2 · ans C] id c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In a blast furnace, iron(III) oxide is reduced by carbon monoxide: \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\,\text{CO} \rightarrow 2\,\text{Fe} + 3\,\text{CO}_2\). A charge of \(64.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\) (molar mass \(160\ \text{g/mol}\)) is heated with \(16.8\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{CO}\) (molar mass \(28\ \text{g/mol}\)), and the run isolates iron at a \(75.0\%\) yield. Using \(56\ \text{g/mol}\) for \(\text{Fe}\), the mass of \(\text{Fe}\) actually recovered is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(33.6\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(22.4\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(16.8\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(5.6\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the limiting reagent is \(\text{CO}\), which caps the theoretical yield at \(22.4\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{Fe}\), and applying the \(75.0\%\) yield gives \(16.8\ \text{g}\) actually recovered. First convert each mass to moles: \(64.0\ \text{g} \div 160\ \text{g/mol} = 0.40\ \text{mol}\ \text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\) and \(16.8\ \text{g} \div 28\ \text{g/mol} = 0.60\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}\). Identify the limiting reagent by dividing each amount by its coefficient: for \(\text{CO}\), \(0.60 \div 3 = 0.20\); for \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\), \(0.40 \div 1 = 0.40\). Because \(0.20 < 0.40\), \(\text{CO}\) limits the reaction even though more grams of the oxide are present. Using the \(3:2\) ratio of \(\text{CO}\) to \(\text{Fe}\): \(0.60\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO} \times \frac{2\ \text{mol}\ \text{Fe}}{3\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}} = 0.40\ \text{mol}\ \text{Fe}\), so the theoretical mass is \(0.40\ \text{mol} \times 56\ \text{g/mol} = 22.4\ \text{g}\). Finally, the \(75.0\%\) yield gives \(22.4\ \text{g} \times 0.750 = 16.8\ \text{g}\). (Choice A) \(33.6\ \text{g}\) results from wrongly treating \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\) as the limiting reagent because it is supplied in greater mass: \(0.40\ \text{mol}\ \text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 \times 2 = 0.80\ \text{mol}\ \text{Fe} = 44.8\ \text{g}\) theoretical, then \(\times 0.750 = 33.6\ \text{g}\); this ignores that the mole-to-coefficient comparison makes \(\text{CO}\) limiting. (Choice B) \(22.4\ \text{g}\) is the theoretical yield obtained from the correct limiting-reagent analysis but with the \(75.0\%\) percent-yield step omitted, so it overstates the mass that the reaction actually delivers. (Choice D) \(5.6\ \text{g}\) comes from applying the \(25\%\) fraction lost rather than the \(75.0\%\) fraction recovered to the correct \(22.4\ \text{g}\) theoretical yield (\(22.4 \times 0.25 = 5.6\)), reversing the meaning of percent yield. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must convert masses to moles, determine the limiting reagent from a mole-to-coefficient comparison, carry the limiting amount through the stoichiometric ratio to a theoretical yield, and then scale that intermediate result by the percent yield.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Selects the reactant with the larger mass as limiting instead of comparing moles divided by coefficients$a$),
  ('c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Stops at the theoretical yield from the correct limiting reagent but omits the final percent-yield multiplication$a$),
  ('c315642b-fc01-4d63-9803-a99847ae3aca', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Multiplies the theoretical yield by the 25% lost fraction rather than the 75% recovered fraction$a$);

-- 11. [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield · Skill 2 · ans B] id c46540a6-e00e-481b-88ee-eaa98800fabe
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(20.0\,\text{g}\) sample of impure magnesite that is \(84.0\%\) \(\text{MgCO}_3\) by mass (the rest inert) is heated to drive \(\text{MgCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{MgO} + \text{CO}_2\). The escaping \(\text{CO}_2\) is trapped, and \(7.04\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{CO}_2\) is collected. Using molar masses \(\text{MgCO}_3 = 84\,\text{g/mol}\), \(\text{MgO} = 40\,\text{g/mol}\), and \(\text{CO}_2 = 44\,\text{g/mol}\), the percent yield of \(\text{CO}_2\) is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(67.2\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(80.0\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(88.0\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(125\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the percent yield must compare the actual \(\text{CO}_2\) collected to the theoretical \(\text{CO}_2\) that the reactive portion of the sample can produce, and reaching that theoretical value requires three chained steps. First, only \(84.0\%\) of the \(20.0\,\text{g}\) sample is reactive \(\text{MgCO}_3\): \(0.840 \times 20.0\,\text{g} = 16.8\,\text{g}\) of \(\text{MgCO}_3\) (the inert remainder produces no gas). Second, this mass is \(\frac{16.8\,\text{g}}{84\,\text{g/mol}} = 0.200\,\text{mol}\) of \(\text{MgCO}_3\), and because the balanced equation has a \(1:1\) mole ratio of \(\text{MgCO}_3\) to \(\text{CO}_2\), the theoretical amount of \(\text{CO}_2\) is \(0.200\,\text{mol}\), or \(0.200\,\text{mol} \times 44\,\text{g/mol} = 8.80\,\text{g}\). Third, percent yield is actual over theoretical times \(100\): \(\frac{7.04\,\text{g}}{8.80\,\text{g}} \times 100 = 80.0\%\). (Choice A) \(67.2\%\) drops the purity constraint and treats the full \(20.0\,\text{g}\) as \(\text{MgCO}_3\), giving \(\frac{20.0\,\text{g}}{84\,\text{g/mol}} = 0.238\,\text{mol}\) and a theoretical \(\text{CO}_2\) of \(0.238\,\text{mol} \times 44\,\text{g/mol} = 10.48\,\text{g}\), so \(\frac{7.04\,\text{g}}{10.48\,\text{g}} \times 100 \approx 67.2\%\); the inert \(16\%\) of the sample contains no carbonate and cannot contribute to the theoretical gas. (Choice C) \(88.0\%\) applies the purity correctly but divides the actual \(\text{CO}_2\) by the theoretical mass of the wrong product, the solid \(\text{MgO}\): \(0.200\,\text{mol} \times 40\,\text{g/mol} = 8.00\,\text{g}\), giving \(\frac{7.04\,\text{g}}{8.00\,\text{g}} \times 100 = 88.0\%\); the species actually measured is \(\text{CO}_2\), so the theoretical denominator must be the \(\text{CO}_2\) mass of \(8.80\,\text{g}\). (Choice D) \(125\%\) inverts the percent-yield ratio to theoretical over actual, \(\frac{8.80\,\text{g}}{7.04\,\text{g}} \times 100\); a value above \(100\%\) is impossible for a yield that loses gas to incomplete decomposition and signals the fraction was placed upside down. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify the controlling purity constraint, carry the intermediate mole quantity through a mole ratio to the correct product, and only then form the percent-yield ratio in the right order.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'c46540a6-e00e-481b-88ee-eaa98800fabe';
