  ('c46540a6-e00e-481b-88ee-eaa98800fabe', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Inverted the percent-yield ratio to theoretical-over-actual, yielding an impossible value above 100%$a$);

-- 12. [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield · Skill 2 · ans A] id d45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Carbon burns in limited oxygen by the balanced equation \(2\,\text{C} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\,\text{CO}\). A vessel is charged with \(18.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{C}\) (molar mass \(12.0\ \text{g/mol}\)) and \(40.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{O}_2\) (molar mass \(32.0\ \text{g/mol}\)), and the reaction is run to completion at a percent yield of \(80.0\%\). Using a molar mass of \(28.0\ \text{g/mol}\) for \(\text{CO}\), the mass of \(\text{CO}\) actually recovered is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(33.6\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(42.0\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(56.0\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(16.8\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the recovered mass is fixed by chaining four results: the limiting reagent sets the moles of \(\text{CO}\), which set the theoretical mass, which the percent yield then scales down. First convert to moles: \(18.0\ \text{g}\ \text{C} \div 12.0\ \text{g/mol} = 1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{C}\) and \(40.0\ \text{g}\ \text{O}_2 \div 32.0\ \text{g/mol} = 1.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{O}_2\). Identify the limiting reagent by dividing each amount by its coefficient in \(2\,\text{C} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\,\text{CO}\): for carbon \(1.5 \div 2 = 0.75\), for oxygen \(1.25 \div 1 = 1.25\); the smaller value, \(0.75\), belongs to carbon, so \(\text{C}\) limits even though it is present in the larger molar amount. The limiting carbon gives \(1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{C} \times \tfrac{2\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}}{2\ \text{mol}\ \text{C}} = 1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}\), a theoretical mass of \(1.5\ \text{mol} \times 28.0\ \text{g/mol} = 42.0\ \text{g}\). Applying the \(80.0\%\) yield, the actual mass recovered is \(42.0\ \text{g} \times 0.800 = 33.6\ \text{g}\). (Choice B) The value \(42.0\ \text{g}\) is the theoretical yield obtained from the limiting carbon but with the \(80.0\%\) percent-yield step omitted; the actual mass recovered must be reduced by that factor to \(33.6\ \text{g}\). (Choice C) The value \(56.0\ \text{g}\) results from misassigning the limiting reagent: treating the reactant of smaller molar amount, \(\text{O}_2\) at \(1.25\ \text{mol}\), as limiting yields \(1.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{O}_2 \times \tfrac{2\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}}{1\ \text{mol}\ \text{O}_2} = 2.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO} = 70.0\ \text{g}\), then \(\times 0.800 = 56.0\ \text{g}\); dividing by the stoichiometric coefficients shows carbon, not oxygen, runs out first. (Choice D) The value \(16.8\ \text{g}\) comes from applying the \(\text{O}_2\) coefficient ratio to carbon, taking \(1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{C} \times \tfrac{1}{2} = 0.75\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO} = 21.0\ \text{g}\) before the \(\times 0.800\) step; the balanced equation produces \(\text{CO}\) and \(\text{C}\) in a \(2:2\) (i.e. \(1:1\)) ratio, so \(1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{C}\) yields \(1.5\ \text{mol}\ \text{CO}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate a limiting-reagent determination, a mole-to-mass conversion, and a percent-yield correction in sequence, carrying each intermediate result forward to reach the recovered mass.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'd45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops at the theoretical yield from the correctly identified limiting reagent (42.0 g) and omits the final percent-yield multiplication.$a$),
  ('d45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Misidentifies the limiting reagent as the species with the smaller molar amount (O2, 1.25 mol) instead of dividing each amount by its stoichiometric coefficient, then carries that wrong limiting reagent through to actual yield (70.0 g x 0.80).$a$),
  ('d45d65b9-b542-440e-8f15-e065583c3505', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Applies the O2 coefficient (1) to carbon, using a 2:1 C:CO ratio so 1.5 mol C gives 0.75 mol CO (21.0 g) instead of the correct 2:2 (1:1) ratio, then multiplies by 0.80.$a$);

-- 13. [Molecules, Moles & Molar Mass · Skill 2 · ans A] id 173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A landscaper scoops \(75.0\,\text{g}\) of a granular fertilizer that is \(88.0\%\) pure ammonium sulfate, \((\text{NH}_4)_2\text{SO}_4\) (molar mass \(132.15\,\text{g/mol}\)), by mass; the remainder is inert sand. How many moles of hydrogen atoms does this scoop most nearly contain?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(4.00\\ \\text{mol H}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(4.54\\ \\text{mol H}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(2.00\\ \\text{mol H}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(1.00\\ \\text{mol H}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the calculation must chain a purity correction, a mass-to-mole conversion, and the correct hydrogen-atom count per formula unit. First, only the pure ammonium sulfate contributes hydrogen, so the inert sand is removed: \(75.0\,\text{g} \times 0.880 = 66.0\,\text{g}\ (\text{NH}_4)_2\text{SO}_4\). Next convert this corrected mass to moles of compound: \(66.0\,\text{g} \div 132.15\,\text{g/mol} = 0.499\,\text{mol}\ (\text{NH}_4)_2\text{SO}_4\). Finally, each formula unit holds two ammonium ions and each \(\text{NH}_4^+\) carries four hydrogen atoms, giving \(2 \times 4 = 8\) hydrogen atoms per formula unit: \(0.499\,\text{mol} \times 8 = 3.99 \approx 4.00\,\text{mol H}\). (Choice B) \(4.54\,\text{mol}\) results from skipping the purity step and dividing the full \(75.0\,\text{g}\) by \(132.15\,\text{g/mol}\) before multiplying by \(8\); this counts the hydrogen as if the inert sand were also ammonium sulfate. (Choice C) \(2.00\,\text{mol}\) results from applying the purity and mass-to-mole steps correctly but then multiplying the \(0.499\,\text{mol}\) of compound by \(4\), reading only the subscript on \(\text{NH}_4\) while overlooking the outer subscript \(2\) that doubles the number of ammonium groups. (Choice D) \(1.00\,\text{mol}\) comes from multiplying the \(0.499\,\text{mol}\) of compound by only \(2\), counting one hydrogen per ammonium ion (or the two ammonium ions themselves) instead of the four hydrogen atoms each ammonium ion contributes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate a purity correction with a mass-to-mole conversion and the atom-to-formula-unit ratio, identifying that only the pure compound contributes hydrogen and that each formula unit supplies eight hydrogen atoms.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Omits the purity step and divides the full sample mass by the molar mass before applying the correct hydrogen multiplier, treating the inert filler as if it were ammonium sulfate.$a$),
  ('173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Parses the ammonium subscript correctly as 4 H but ignores the outer subscript 2, undercounting the hydrogen atoms per formula unit by a factor of two.$a$),
  ('173155a2-474b-4228-b536-57c7345a4fd6', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Counts only two hydrogen-bearing units per formula (the two ammonium ions) instead of the eight hydrogen atoms, multiplying moles of compound by 2 rather than 8.$a$);

-- 14. [Molecules, Moles & Molar Mass · Skill 2 · ans B] id 688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A technician sets out four separate \(50.0\,\text{g}\) samples, one each of \(\text{Al}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3\), \(\text{Ca}(\text{NO}_3)_2\), \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\), and \(\text{Zn}(\text{NO}_3)_2\). Using atomic weights \(\text{Al}=27.0\), \(\text{S}=32.1\), \(\text{O}=16.0\), \(\text{N}=14.0\), \(\text{Na}=23.0\), \(\text{C}=12.0\), \(\text{Zn}=65.4\), and \(\text{Ca}=40.1\,\text{g/mol}\), the sample that delivers the greatest number of moles of oxygen atoms is most likely the$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3\\) sample (\\(M = 342.3\\,\\text{g/mol}\\))."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Ca}(\\text{NO}_3)_2\\) sample (\\(M = 164.1\\,\\text{g/mol}\\))."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3\\) sample (\\(M = 106.0\\,\\text{g/mol}\\))."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Zn}(\\text{NO}_3)_2\\) sample (\\(M = 189.4\\,\\text{g/mol}\\))."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because moles of oxygen atoms equals (mass ÷ molar mass) × (oxygen atoms per formula unit), so a high oxygen subscript only wins if it is not cancelled by a large molar mass, and the parenthetical subscript must be applied exactly once to each oxygen-bearing group to count oxygen correctly. For \(\text{Ca}(\text{NO}_3)_2\), the subscript \(2\) outside the parentheses gives \(2\times3 = 6\) oxygen atoms; its molar mass is \(40.1 + 2(14.0) + 6(16.0) = 164.1\,\text{g/mol}\), so \(50.0\,\text{g}\) is \(50.0/164.1 = 0.305\,\text{mol}\), and \(0.305 \times 6 = 1.83\,\text{mol O}\). Running the same chain for the others: \(\text{Al}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3\) has \(12\) oxygen atoms and \(M = 342.3\), giving \((50.0/342.3)\times12 = 1.75\,\text{mol O}\); \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\) has \(3\) oxygen atoms and \(M = 106.0\), giving \((50.0/106.0)\times3 = 1.42\,\text{mol O}\); and \(\text{Zn}(\text{NO}_3)_2\) has \(2\times3 = 6\) oxygen atoms and \(M = 65.4 + 2(14.0) + 6(16.0) = 189.4\), giving \((50.0/189.4)\times6 = 1.58\,\text{mol O}\). The largest value is \(1.83\,\text{mol O}\), so \(\text{Ca}(\text{NO}_3)_2\) delivers the most oxygen even though it carries neither the highest oxygen count nor the lowest molar mass. (Choice A) \(\text{Al}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3\) holds the most oxygen atoms per formula unit (\(12\)), but its large molar mass of \(342.3\,\text{g/mol}\) means \(50.0\,\text{g}\) supplies only \(0.146\,\text{mol}\) of formula units, so the oxygen total falls to \(1.75\,\text{mol}\); selecting it follows from ranking by oxygen atoms per unit while ignoring that molar mass divides the mole count down. (Choice C) \(\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3\) has the smallest molar mass (\(106.0\,\text{g/mol}\)) and therefore the most formula units in \(50.0\,\text{g}\) (\(0.472\,\text{mol}\)), but it carries only \(3\) oxygen atoms per unit, giving just \(1.42\,\text{mol O}\); choosing it applies a "smallest molar mass yields the most moles" shortcut that ignores the differing oxygen counts. (Choice D) \(\text{Zn}(\text{NO}_3)_2\) actually contains \(2\times3 = 6\) oxygen atoms and yields \(1.58\,\text{mol O}\); reaching it as the winner requires applying the external subscript \(2\) a second time to the already-distributed oxygens and counting \(12\) oxygen atoms instead of \(6\), which inflates the result to \((50.0/189.4)\times12 = 3.17\,\text{mol O}\) and makes the moderate molar mass appear to deliver the most oxygen. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a molar-mass calculation, a per-formula-unit oxygen count, and a mass-to-mole conversion across four candidates and then compare the results to identify which constraint governs the maximum.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Ranks candidates by oxygen atoms per formula unit (picks the 12-oxygen species) while ignoring that the large molar mass divides the mole count down$a$),
  ('688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Applies the 'smallest molar mass gives the most moles' shortcut, ignoring that oxygen atoms per formula unit differ between compounds$a$),
  ('688df2f0-5334-4ab0-b08e-355a146851a4', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Over-applies the outer parenthetical subscript, counting 12 oxygen atoms instead of 6 for Zn(NO3)2, which inflates its mol O to 3.17 and makes it appear to be the maximum$a$);

-- 15. [Molecules, Moles & Molar Mass · Skill 2 · ans D] id 9a66fc96-c9a0-47f7-b07a-bd8c8e082750
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A technician needs a sample of solid calcium phosphate, \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\) (molar mass \(310.18\ \text{g/mol}\)), that supplies the same number of moles of oxygen atoms as \(51.0\ \text{g}\) of sodium nitrate, \(\text{NaNO}_3\) (molar mass \(85.0\ \text{g/mol}\)). What mass of \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\) is most nearly required?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(51.0\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(139.6\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(186.1\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(69.8\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the target quantity (moles of oxygen atoms) must be carried through both compounds, which have different numbers of oxygen atoms per formula unit. First convert the nitrate mass to moles of compound: \(\frac{51.0\ \text{g}}{85.0\ \text{g/mol}} = 0.600\ \text{mol}\ \text{NaNO}_3\). Each formula unit of \(\text{NaNO}_3\) contains \(3\) oxygen atoms, so the sample provides \(0.600 \times 3 = 1.80\ \text{mol O}\). To match this oxygen amount with calcium phosphate, divide by the \(2 \times 4 = 8\) oxygen atoms per formula unit of \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\): \(\frac{1.80\ \text{mol O}}{8} = 0.225\ \text{mol}\ \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\). Finally convert back to mass: \(0.225\ \text{mol} \times 310.18\ \text{g/mol} \approx 69.8\ \text{g}\). (Choice A) \(51.0\ \text{g}\) assumes that equal masses of two different compounds contribute equal moles of oxygen atoms; this ignores both the differing molar masses and the differing oxygen counts per formula unit and simply restates the given nitrate mass. (Choice B) \(139.6\ \text{g}\) results from counting only the \(4\) oxygen atoms inside a single \(\text{PO}_4\) group and ignoring the subscript \(2\) outside the parentheses, giving \(\frac{1.80}{4} = 0.450\ \text{mol}\) and \(0.450 \times 310.18\). (Choice C) \(186.1\ \text{g}\) results from matching the moles of the nitrate compound itself (\(0.600\ \text{mol}\)) to moles of calcium phosphate, \(0.600 \times 310.18\), omitting both oxygen-atom conversions that the equivalence requires. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a mass-to-mole conversion, two distinct atoms-per-formula-unit ratios, and a mole-to-mass conversion, carrying the intermediate oxygen-atom amount across two compounds to identify the controlling quantity.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '9a66fc96-c9a0-47f7-b07a-bd8c8e082750';
