-- 17. [Types of Reactions · Skill 2 · ans C] id d339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A clean strip of zinc weighing \(6.54\,\text{g}\) is dropped into a beaker holding \(0.060\,\text{mol}\) of \(\text{Ag}^+\) and \(0.200\,\text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) in solution. The activity series runs (most to least active) \(\text{Zn} > \text{Cu} > \text{Ag}\), so zinc can reduce either cation, and the more readily reduced cation deposits first. Using molar masses \(\text{Zn}=65.4\), \(\text{Cu}=63.5\), and \(\text{Ag}=107.9\,\text{g/mol}\), the total mass of metal that will most likely plate onto the strip once the zinc is fully consumed is closest to$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(6.35\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(6.47\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(10.92\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(9.01\\,\\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the problem chains a single-displacement selectivity judgment to a two-stage limiting-reagent calculation. First convert the strip: \(6.54\,\text{g} \div 65.4\,\text{g/mol} = 0.100\,\text{mol Zn}\). Because \(\text{Ag}\) is the LEAST active metal, \(\text{Ag}^+\) is the most readily reduced cation and is displaced first by the half-reaction \(\text{Zn} + 2\,\text{Ag}^+ \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag}\). Reducing all \(0.060\,\text{mol Ag}^+\) consumes \(0.060/2 = 0.030\,\text{mol Zn}\) and deposits \(0.060\,\text{mol Ag}\). That leaves \(0.100 - 0.030 = 0.070\,\text{mol Zn}\), which then reduces \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) by \(\text{Zn} + \text{Cu}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} + \text{Cu}\) (1:1); since \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is in large excess (\(0.200\,\text{mol}\)), the leftover zinc is limiting and deposits \(0.070\,\text{mol Cu}\). Total plated mass \(= (0.060)(107.9) + (0.070)(63.5) = 6.47 + 4.45 = 10.92\,\text{g}\). (Choice A) \(6.35\,\text{g}\) results from reversing the selectivity and sending all \(0.100\,\text{mol Zn}\) to \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) (which is present in excess), giving \(0.100\,\text{mol Cu} \times 63.5 = 6.35\,\text{g}\); this ignores that the least-active metal's cation, \(\text{Ag}^+\), is reduced preferentially. (Choice B) \(6.47\,\text{g}\) is the mass of silver alone, obtained by stopping after the first stage and overlooking that \(0.070\,\text{mol}\) of zinc remains to reduce \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\). (Choice D) \(9.01\,\text{g}\) comes from treating the silver displacement as 1:1 (\(\text{Zn} + \text{Ag}^+\)) instead of 1:2, which wrongly consumes \(0.060\,\text{mol Zn}\) on silver and leaves only \(0.040\,\text{mol Zn}\) for copper: \((0.060)(107.9) + (0.040)(63.5) = 9.01\,\text{g}\); the \(+1\) charge on \(\text{Ag}^+\) requires two silver ions per zinc atom. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must rank two displacement reactions by activity, then propagate a limiting-reagent result from the first reaction into the stoichiometry of the second to reach a final mass.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'd339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the displacement selectivity by reducing the excess Cu2+ instead of the least-active metal's cation Ag+, sending all zinc to copper$a$),
  ('d339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops after the first displacement stage, reporting only the plated silver and ignoring the leftover zinc that goes on to reduce Cu2+$a$),
  ('d339e23a-25b7-43a9-b909-31c05f0d04c0', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses 1:1 instead of 1:2 Zn:Ag+ stoichiometry, ignoring the +1 charge that demands two silver ions per zinc atom$a$);

-- 18. [Types of Reactions · Skill 2 · ans B] id e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$When chlorine gas is bubbled into HOT, CONCENTRATED aqueous \(\text{NaOH}\), it disproportionates to chloride and a single chlorine oxyanion, with \(\text{O}\) at \(-2\) and \(\text{Na}\) at \(+1\) throughout. If \(0.60\,\text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cl}_2\) reacts completely under these conditions, the mass of sodium chlorate produced is most nearly (molar masses, in \(\text{g/mol}\): \(\text{NaClO}_3 = 106.4\), \(\text{NaClO} = 74.4\))$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(10.6\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(21.3\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(14.9\\,\\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(63.9\\,\\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the "hot, concentrated" condition is the controlling constraint: unlike cold dilute hydroxide (which gives hypochlorite, \(\text{OCl}^-\), with \(\text{Cl}\) at \(+1\)), hot concentrated hydroxide drives chlorine all the way to the higher oxyanion chlorate, \(\text{ClO}_3^-\). In \(\text{ClO}_3^-\), three \(\text{O}\) at \(-2\) with an overall \(-1\) charge give \(\text{Cl} + 3(-2) = -1\), so \(\text{Cl} = +5\), and the balanced disproportionation is \(3\,\text{Cl}_2 + 6\,\text{NaOH} \rightarrow 5\,\text{NaCl} + \text{NaClO}_3 + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). The product chlorate appears in a fixed \(3{:}1\) ratio to \(\text{Cl}_2\), so \(0.60\,\text{mol Cl}_2 \times \frac{1\,\text{mol NaClO}_3}{3\,\text{mol Cl}_2} = 0.20\,\text{mol NaClO}_3\). Converting to mass, \(0.20\,\text{mol} \times 106.4\,\text{g/mol} = 21.3\,\text{g}\). (Choice A) \(10.6\,\text{g}\) corresponds to \(0.10\,\text{mol NaClO}_3\), obtained by dividing the \(0.60\,\text{mol Cl}_2\) by \(6\) (the \(\text{NaOH}\) coefficient) instead of by \(3\) (the \(\text{Cl}_2\) coefficient), so the wrong balancing coefficient is used to set the chlorate ratio. (Choice C) \(14.9\,\text{g}\) keeps the correct \(0.20\,\text{mol}\) of chlorate but multiplies by the molar mass of hypochlorite \(\text{NaClO}\) (\(74.4\,\text{g/mol}\)) rather than chlorate \(\text{NaClO}_3\) (\(106.4\,\text{g/mol}\)); this is the wrong oxyanion for the stated hot, concentrated conditions. (Choice D) \(63.9\,\text{g}\) corresponds to \(0.60\,\text{mol NaClO}_3\), obtained by assuming an unbalanced \(1{:}1\) split in which every mole of \(\text{Cl}_2\) yields a mole of chlorate, ignoring that the balanced equation produces only \(1\,\text{mol NaClO}_3\) per \(3\,\text{mol Cl}_2\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify which reaction condition governs the product, assign the resulting oxidation state, balance the disproportionation, and chain the mole ratio into a mass conversion.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$divides Cl2 by the NaOH coefficient (6) instead of the Cl2 coefficient (3) when applying the chlorate mole ratio$a$),
  ('e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$correct chlorate moles but multiplied by the hypochlorite (NaClO) molar mass instead of the chlorate (NaClO3) molar mass$a$),
  ('e726befc-0dea-4c24-9b69-9ee7eb584777', 'D', 'misconception', $a$assumes an unbalanced 1:1 Cl2-to-chlorate ratio, ignoring the 3:1 stoichiometry of the balanced equation$a$);

-- 19. [Types of Reactions · Skill 2 · ans A] id ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$The thermite reaction proceeds as \(8\,\text{Al}(s) + 3\,\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4(s) \rightarrow 4\,\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3(s) + 9\,\text{Fe}(s)\), where magnetite, \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\), can be regarded as one \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) and two \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) per formula unit. For this reaction, how many electrons are most likely gained by one formula unit of the oxidizing agent?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(8\\) electrons, gained by \\(\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(9\\) electrons, gained by \\(\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(6\\) electrons, gained by \\(\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(3\\) electrons, gained by \\(\\text{Al}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the oxidizing agent must first be identified and then its per-formula-unit electron gain must be computed from the actual oxidation states. The oxidizing agent is the species that is itself reduced. Aluminum goes from \(0\) in \(\text{Al}(s)\) to \(+3\) in \(\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3\), so aluminum is oxidized and \(\text{Al}\) is the reducing agent. Iron is reduced: oxygen is \(-2\) throughout, and in \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\) the iron averages \(+8/3\), realized as one \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) and two \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\), all dropping to \(0\) in elemental \(\text{Fe}(s)\). Therefore \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\) is the oxidizing agent. Counting the electrons gained by one \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\) unit requires honoring the mixed oxidation states: the single \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) gains \(2\) electrons and the two \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) ions gain \(3\) each for \(2 \times 3 = 6\), so the total is \(2 + 6 = 8\) electrons. This is internally consistent with the overall stoichiometry: \(8\) Al atoms each lose \(3\) electrons for \(24\) electrons released, and \(3\) \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\) units each gain \(8\) for \(24\) electrons accepted. (Choice B) A value of \(9\) results from treating all three iron atoms in \(\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4\) as \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) and computing \(3 \times 3 = 9\), which ignores that magnetite contains one \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) and overstates the charge that must be neutralized. (Choice C) A value of \(6\) results from treating all three iron atoms as \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\), computing \(3 \times 2 = 6\); this confuses magnetite with iron(II) oxide, \(\text{FeO}\), and undercounts the electrons accepted by the two iron(III) centers. (Choice D) A value of \(3\) electrons gained by \(\text{Al}\) misassigns the oxidizing agent: aluminum is oxidized from \(0\) to \(+3\), so it loses three electrons and is the reducing agent, not the species that gains electrons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify the controlling oxidation states within a mixed-valence oxide and chain that determination to a multi-step electron count for the correct species.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Treats all Fe in magnetite as Fe(3+), ignoring the mixed +2/+3 valence, yielding 3 x 3 = 9 electrons$a$),
  ('ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Treats all Fe in magnetite as Fe(2+) (confuses Fe3O4 with FeO), yielding 3 x 2 = 6 electrons$a$),
  ('ec8077f3-2135-4753-8148-00b6ad148a61', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Identifies the reducing agent (Al, which loses 3 electrons) as the species that gains electrons, reversing oxidizing/reducing roles$a$);

